    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d602">

                <pb facs="1119" n="1103" sameAs="frus:frus1950v04/pg1103"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">511.6121/2–1450: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Kirk</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d602-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-02-14T18:00:00+03:00">February 14,
                            1950—6 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>528. Embtel 319, January 30.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d602-f2"/> Following is text in translation of letter received from
                        Ramsin,<anchor n="3" xml:id="d602-f3"/> Soyuzpechat, dated February 11.</p>
                <p>Mezhdunarodnaya Kniga directed to us your letter of January 30 concerning
                    distribution of magazine <hi rendition="#italic">Amerika</hi> in 1949.</p>
                <p>In accordance with request expressed in letter, we inform you that magazine <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Amerika</hi> is sold in more than 70 cities of Soviet
                    Union, including all largest centers of country. In every one of these cities
                    outside Moscow magazine <hi rendition="#italic">Amerika</hi> is on sale from 3
                    to 50 kiosks of Soyuzpechat, depending on size of city.</p>
                <p>600 copies of each number of magazine <hi rendition="#italic">Amerika</hi> are
                    sent regularly to City of Vladivostok for sale.</p>
                <p>Thus, your assertions that distribution of magazine <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Amerika</hi> has been ineffective, that measures have not been taken for
                    distribution of magazine in whole country, and that magazine was absent from
                    sale outside Moscow, are without foundation.</p>
                <p>Adjoined, I send you information on unsold copies remaining from magazine <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Amerika</hi> for year 1949.</p>
                <p>Enclosure indicates following unsold copies <hi rendition="#italic">Amerika:</hi>
                        25–35<anchor n="4" xml:id="d602-f4"/> during 1949; 25, 1063; 26, 1844; 27,
                    5954; 28, 8143; 29, 12337; 30, 15129; 31, 19469; 32, 17165; 33, 21208; 34,
                    26130; 35, 23020.</p>
                <pb facs="1120" n="1104" xml:id="pg1104"/>
                <p>Present situation: Issue 35 on sale December 25, paid for January 10. 36 on sale
                    since February 2, not paid for. Issue 37 delivered Soyuzpechat February 7.
                    Soyuzpechat presumably expect begin turnbacks on issue 36.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d602-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d602-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram was relayed to Frankfort at 2:57 p. m. on February 14.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d602-f2" n="2" xml:id="d602-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed; but see footnote 5, <ref target="#pg1080">p. 1080</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d602-f3" n="3" xml:id="d602-f3-content">
                <p>Pëtr (Peter) Borisovich Ramsin was the head of Soyuzpechat.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d602-f4" n="4" xml:id="d602-f4-content">
                <p>These figures indicate the issue number of <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >Amerika</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
